# iio-sensor-proxy with libssc on pipa (Xiaomi Pad 6, sm8250 SLPI/SDSP)
#
# This file SHADOWS the rule shipped by the COPR `pipa-sensors` package
# (/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/81-libssc-xiaomi-pipa.rules). Same name in /etc
# wins, so the package rule is inert. Why:
#   * the package rule sets ACCEL_MOUNT_MATRIX on ALL fastrpc-* devices and
#     never tags the sensor types, so the accelerometer is never claimed and
#     a second matrix would be appended if both rules ran.
#
# Upstream 80-iio-sensor-proxy.rules only enables "ssc-light ssc-compass"
# for fastrpc-sdsp. The accelerometer (and proximity) must be opted in
# per-device — the SSC accelerometer open can block on some devices, so
# upstream keeps it behind an explicit tag (see liuqin/ArchPad).
#
# ssc-accel = auto-rotation (BMI3x0 IMU). Deliberately NOT tagging
# ssc-proximity: pipa has no proximity hardware (the firmware registry only
# carries bmi3x0 + ak0991x), and a bogus tag makes the proxy claim and drop a
# nonexistent sensor on every start (the "--- Proximity sensor disappeared"
# churn in monitor-sensor). ssc-light/ssc-compass stay as upstream's
# 80-iio-sensor-proxy.rules sets them.

# Bind the sensor-DSP FastRPC tunnel to the device: whenever
# /dev/fastrpc-sdsp exists, hexagonrpcd-sdsp is started via systemd.
SUBSYSTEM=="misc", KERNEL=="fastrpc-sdsp", TAG+="systemd", \
  ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="hexagonrpcd-sdsp.service", \
  ENV{IIO_SENSOR_PROXY_TYPE}+="ssc-accel", \
  ENV{ACCEL_MOUNT_MATRIX}="-1, 0, 0; 0, -1, 0; 0, 0, -1"

# ACCEL_MOUNT_MATRIX notes (landscape-first panel, tested by ArchPad on pipa):
#   rotation dead/wrong? rotate the tablet and check monitor-sensor; swap the
#   matrix for "-1, 0, 0; 0, -1, 0; 0, 0, 1" if the reading is mirrored.
